/*
 * LC 202 — Happy Number
 *
 * PROBLEM:
 * A happy number is defined by the following process:
 * - Start with any positive integer n
 * - Replace n by sum of squares of its digits
 * - Repeat until n equals 1 (happy) or loops endlessly (unhappy)
 * Return true if n is happy, false otherwise.
 *
 * CONSTRAINTS:
 * - 1 <= n <= 2^31 - 1
 * - No overflow risk — after first step, n drops to max 810
 *   (max digit 9, max 10 digits: 9² × 10 = 810)
 *   From 810 onwards number never grows large enough to overflow int
 *   → int is safe, long is unnecessary
 *
 * CORE INSIGHT — THIS IS A CYCLE DETECTION PROBLEM:
 * Happy number → sequence eventually reaches 1
 * Unhappy number → sequence gets stuck in infinite cycle, never reaches 1
 * Example cycle: 2→4→16→37→58→89→145→42→20→4→ (back to 4, cycle!)
 * "Have we seen this number before?" → HashSet existence check
 *
 * ALL APPROACHES:
 * ┌──────────────────┬─────────────────────────────┬──────────┬──────────┐
 * │ Approach         │ Idea                        │ TC       │ SC       │
 * ├──────────────────┼─────────────────────────────┼──────────┼──────────┤
 * │ HashSet          │ Store seen results          │ O(log n) │ O(log n) │
 * │ Floyd's Cycle    │ Fast & slow pointers        │ O(log n) │ O(1)     │
 * └──────────────────┴─────────────────────────────┴──────────┴──────────┘
 *
 * WHY TC is O(log n) not O(n):
 * Numbers shrink rapidly after each step.
 * A k-digit number has sum of squares at most 81k.
 * 81k has at most 3 digits → numbers reduce to 3 digits quickly.
 * Cycle length within 3-digit numbers is bounded → O(log n) total steps.
 *
 * FLOYD'S CYCLE — not coded (requires Linked List pattern knowledge)
 * Fast pointer takes 2 steps, slow takes 1 step per iteration.
 * If they meet → cycle. If fast reaches 1 → happy.
 * SC: O(1) — no extra space needed. Revisit after Linked List session.
 *
 * ─────────────────────────────────────────
 * APPROACH 1 — HASHSET (Optimal for now)
 * ─────────────────────────────────────────
 * WHY HashSet:
 * Need O(1) existence check — "have we seen this sum before?"
 * Same pattern as Contains Duplicate — HashSet cycle detection.
 *
 * HOW:
 * Outer loop — while set.add(n) returns true:
 *   set.add(n) returns false when n already exists → cycle detected → exit
 *   No need for explicit contains() check — add() return value handles it
 *
 * WHEN DOES OUTER LOOP EXIT? Two cases:
 *   Case 1: n=1 added to set, next iteration sum=1 again
 *           set.add(1) returns false → exit → return n==1 → true ✅
 *   Case 2: some intermediate sum seen before
 *           set.add(sum) returns false → exit → return n==1 → false ✅
 * return n==1 elegantly handles BOTH exit cases with one statement.
 *
 * Inner loop — digit extraction:
 *   while n > 0:
 *     digit = n % 10    → extract last digit
 *     sum += digit²     → square and accumulate
 *     n /= 10           → remove last digit
 *
 * Dry run — n=19 (happy):
 *   add(19)→true:  9²+1²=82  → n=82
 *   add(82)→true:  2²+8²=68  → n=68
 *   add(68)→true:  8²+6²=100 → n=100
 *   add(100)→true: 0²+0²+1²=1→ n=1
 *   add(1)→true:   1²=1      → n=1
 *   add(1)→FALSE   → exit loop
 *   return n==1 → true ✅
 *
 * Dry run — n=2 (unhappy):
 *   2→4→16→37→58→89→145→42→20→4
 *   add(4)→FALSE (seen before) → exit loop
 *   return n==1 → false ✅
 *
 * TC: O(log n) — numbers shrink rapidly, bounded cycle length
 * SC: O(log n) — HashSet stores intermediate sums until cycle found
 *
 * Edge cases:
 * - n=1 → add(1)→true, sum=1, add(1)→false → return true ✅
 * - n=7 → known happy number, reaches 1 eventually ✅
 */
public boolean isHappy(int n) {
    Set<Integer> set = new HashSet<>();
    // loop exits when n is seen again (cycle) or n=1 seen twice
    while (set.add(n)) {
        int sum = 0;
        // inner loop — extract each digit, square and accumulate
        while (n > 0) {
            int digit = n % 10;   // last digit
            sum += digit * digit; // square it
            n /= 10;              // remove last digit
        }
        n = sum; // replace n with sum of squares
    }
    // n==1 → happy | n!=1 → caught in cycle → unhappy
    return n == 1;
}
